QB.add(49,[{"n":1,"q":"A 35-year-old male presents with complaints of drooping of the right upper eyelid, constriction of the right pupil, and lack of sweating on the right side of the face. On further questioning, the patient admits to experiencing neck pain following a minor car accident a week ago. Physical examination confirms the presence of ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis on the right side of the face. What is the most likely site of the lesion?","o":[{"t":"A) Cervical spinal cord injury (C8-T1)"},{"t":"B) Internal carotid artery dissection"},{"t":"C) Pancoast tumor at the apex of the lung"},{"t":"D) Lesion of the superior orbital fissure"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient presents with Horner's syndrome , a neurological condition characterized by the triad of ptosis (drooping eyelid), miosis (constricted pupil), and anhidrosis (lack of sweating), all on the ipsilateral side."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Horner's syndrome results from disruption of the sympathetic nerves supplying the eye and face."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this case, the combination of Horner's syndrome and recent neck trauma raises suspicion for internal carotid artery dissection, a known cause of Horner’s syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The sympathetic fibers that innervate the eye travel along the internal carotid artery as part of the third-order neurons ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A dissection of this artery can compromise these fibers, leading to the classic triad of ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis. This is an important cause of Horner’s syndrome, especially after neck trauma. Carotid dissection also carries a risk of ischemic stroke due to thromboembolism."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Horner’s Syndrome:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Horner’s syndrome occurs due to interruption of the sympathetic pathway to the eye and face. The sympathetic supply to the eye is a three-neuron pathway :"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features of Horner's Syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ptosis : Drooping of the upper eyelid due to loss of sympathetic innervation to the superior tarsal muscle (also called Muller's muscle)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Miosis : Constricted pupil due to unopposed action of the parasympathetic system following loss of sympathetic input to the dilator pupillae muscle ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anhidrosis : Lack of sweating on the affected side of the face due to involvement of sympathetic fibers supplying the sweat glands."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes of Horner's Syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"First-order neuron lesions : Brainstem stroke, multiple sclerosis, spinal cord trauma (e.g., syringomyelia)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Second-order neuron lesions : Pancoast tumors, thoracic aortic aneurysm, neck surgery."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Third-order neuron lesions : Internal carotid artery dissection, cavernous sinus lesions."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In cases of suspected internal carotid artery dissection, imaging such as CT angiography or MR angiography is crucial to confirm the diagnosis. Treatment involves anticoagulation or antiplatelet therapy to prevent stroke."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"A lesion in the cervical spinal cord (C8-T1) (Option A) affects the first-order neurons of the sympathetic pathway."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While this could cause Horner's syndrome, it presents with other signs of spinal cord injury, such as motor or sensory deficits in the arms and hands, which are not seen in this patient."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Additionally, the patient's history points to neck trauma, and the clinical presentation is more suggestive of a vascular etiology."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"A Pancoast tumor (Option C) , located at the apex of the lung, can compress the sympathetic chain, leading to Horner's syndrome ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This type of tumor disrupts the second-order neurons ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient’s acute presentation after trauma, absence of respiratory symptoms, and additional symptoms like shoulder pain, arm weakness, and weight loss make this diagnosis less likely."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"A lesion at the superior orbital fissure (Option D) would affect the structures that pass through it, including cranial nerves (CN III, IV, V1, and VI), but would not result in Horner's syndrome. This type of lesion may lead to ophthalmoplegia (paralysis of the extraocular muscles), but the patient's symptoms specifically suggest a disruption of the sympathetic nervous system."}]},{"n":2,"q":"A 58-year-old man presents to the clinic with the complaints of right sided weakness and difficulty speaking. His speech is slow, effortful, and fragmented with short sentences, but his comprehension is not affected. Which of the following type of aphasia is this patient suffering from?","o":[{"t":"A) Wernicke’s Aphasia"},{"t":"B) Broca’s Aphasia"},{"t":"C) Global Aphasia"},{"t":"D) Conduction Aphasia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Broca’s aphasia, also called non-fluent aphasia, is caused by damage to the left frontal lobe, specifically the Broca’s area (located in the posterior part of the frontal lobe). Patients with Broca’s aphasia have non-fluent speech, often with grammatical errors and difficulty forming full sentences, but their comprehension remains intact. They may also have right-sided weakness,as seen in this patient."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Wernicke’s Aphasia (Option A) :"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Wernicke’s aphasia is characterized by fluent speech with poor comprehension and the use of nonsensical or irrelevant words (word salad). People with Wernicke’s aphasia can speak in long sentences, but their speech lacks meaning. The patient in the case has non-fluent speech , which points more toward Broca's aphasia, not Wernicke's."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Global Aphasia (Option C) :"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Global aphasia involves severe impairments in both speech production and comprehension. Patients with global aphasia have difficulty with almost all aspects of language, including speaking, understanding, reading, and writing. This patient’s preserved comprehension rules out global aphasia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Conduction Aphasia (Option D) :"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Conduction aphasia is caused by damage to the arcuate fasciculus , the pathway that connects Broca's area and Wernicke’s area. People with conduction aphasia have fluent speech but have difficulty with repetition and making corrections to their speech, often with phonemic paraphasias (incorrect sounds or words). The patient described here does not show the characteristics of fluent speech or frequent paraphasias, so this diagnosis is less likely."},{"k":"img","v":7485}]},{"n":3,"q":"A 48-year-old male presents with acute onset weakness of the right side of the body. On examination: Right-sided complete hemiplegia Right-sided ptosis and dilated pupil Eye deviated outward and downward No sensory deficits Normal consciousness level Which type of hemiplegia is most likely in this patient?","o":[{"t":"A) Weber's hemiplegia"},{"t":"B) Medial medullary hemiplegia"},{"t":"C) Millard-Gubler hemiplegia"},{"t":"D) Pure motor hemiplegia (capsular)"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hemiplegia refers to complete paralysis of one half of the body, resulting from an upper motor neuron lesion affecting the corticospinal tract at any level from cortex to medulla, before its decussation."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Type of Hemipleg Location Key Features Additional Details ia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Weber's Hemiple Midbrain (crus CN III palsy + contralateral Ptosis, dilated pupil, gia cerebri) hemiplegia outward/downward eye"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"deviation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Medial Medullary Medial medulla CN XII palsy + contralatera Tongue deviation to Hemiplegia l hemiplegia ipsilateral side (Option B)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Millard-Gubler Pons CN VI, VII palsy + Facial weakness and Hemiplegia contralateral hemiplegia lateral rectus palsy (Option C)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pure Motor Internal capsule Only motor deficits No cranial nerve Hemiplegia involvement (Option D)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cortical Level Motor cortex Segmental weakness, MCA territory most Hemiplegia (precentral gyrus) often associated with commonly involved"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cortical signs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Initial flaccid paralysis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypotonia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Poor voluntary movement"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Development of spasticity"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Return of reflexes, becoming hyperactive"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Babinski sign appears"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Basic synergistic patterns emerge"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Established spasticity"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sequential recovery: Proximal muscles before distal Lower limb before upper limb Extensor muscle groups before flexors"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Proximal muscles before distal"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lower limb before upper limb"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Extensor muscle groups before flexors"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Synergistic movements may persist"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Proximal muscles before distal"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lower limb before upper limb"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Extensor muscle groups before flexors"}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 65-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with dizziness, difficulty swallowing, and right-sided facial numbness. On examination, she has ataxia, and when asked to stick out her tongue, it deviates to the right. Additionally, she experiences nystagmus and has a diminished gag reflex. What type of stroke is this patient most likely experiencing?","o":[{"t":"A) Weber Syndrome"},{"t":"B) Wallenberg Syndrome"},{"t":"C) Foville Syndrome"},{"t":"D) Medial Medullary Syndrome"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient’s symptoms—dizziness, ataxia, ipsilateral facial numbness, dysphagia, nystagmus, and diminished gag reflex—are characteristic of Wallenberg Syndrome (Lateral Medullary Syndrome) , which results from a lateral medullary infarction."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Medial Medullary Syndrome Lateral Medullary Syndrome (Wallenberg Syndrome) (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"On side of lesion On side of lesion Paralysis with atrophy of one-half half the tongue: Pain, numbness, impaired sensation over one-half the Ipsilateral twelfth nerve face: Descending tract and nucleus fifth nerve"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Ataxia of limbs, falling to side of lesion: Restiform body, cerebellar hemisphere, cerebellar fibers, spinocerebellar tract Nystagmus, diplopia, oscillopsia, vertigo, nausea, vomiting: Vestibular nucleus Horner’s syndrome (miosis, ptosis, decreased sweating): Descending sympathetic tract Dysphagia, hoarseness, paralysis of palate, paralysis of vocal cord, diminished gag reflex: Issuing fibers ninth and tenth nerves Loss of taste: Nucleus and tractus solitarius Numbness of ipsilateral arm, trunk, or leg: Cuneate and gracile nuclei Weakness of lower face: Genuflected upper motor neuron fibers to ipsilateral facial nucleus"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"On side opposite lesion On side opposite lesion Paralysis of arm and leg, sparing face; impaired tactile Impaired pain and thermal sense over half the body, and proprioceptive sense over one-half the body: sometimes face: Spinothalamic tract Contralateral pyramidal tract and medial lemniscus"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Weber Syndrome (Option A) is caused by a ventral midbrain infarction, leading to contralateral hemiparesis and ipsilateral oculomotor nerve palsy, but it does not present with the ataxia or dysphagia seen in this case."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Foville Syndrome (Option C) involves a pontine infarction, resulting in contralateral hemiparesis and ipsilateral abducens palsy, along with facial sensory deficits, but lacks the ataxia and swallowing difficulties seen here."}]},{"n":5,"q":"A 72-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after he collapsed at his home, and the neurologist suspects a medullary stroke after a thorough evaluation. Which of the following is NOT a feature of a medullary stroke?","o":[{"t":"A) Irregular breathing patterns"},{"t":"B) Dysphagia and hoarseness"},{"t":"C) Contralateral muscle weakness"},{"t":"D) Ipsilateral facial pain and loss of temperature sensation"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Contralateral muscle weakness is typically associated with lesions higher in the brainstem or in the cortex, while medullary strokes primarily result in ipsilateral deficits, including facial pain and dysphagia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Medullary stroke"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Medullary strokes involve infarctions in the medulla oblongata, which is critical for various autonomic, sensory, and motor functions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The medulla is supplied by paramedian bulbar arteries (from vertebral arteries and anterior spinal artery) for the medial medulla, and lateral bulbar arteries (from vertebral artery or posterior inferior cerebellar artery) for the lateral medulla."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features: Cardiovascular-Respiratory Centers : Disruption causes unstable blood pressure and irregular breathing. (Option A ruled out) Nucleus of the Solitary Tract (NTS) : Impairment leads to altered baroreflex, respiratory issues, and loss of taste. Area Postrema : Damage causes nausea, vomiting, and reduced gag reflex. Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus : Lesion results in ipsilateral facial pain and loss of temperature/pain sensation. (Option D ruled out) Inferior Olivary Nucleus : Affected motor coordination leads to ataxia and tremors. Pyramidal Decussation : Damage causes contralateral muscle weakness or paralysis. Cuneate and Gracile Nuclei : Lesion causes loss of proprioception and fine touch on the ipsilateral side. Medial Lemniscus : Impairment leads to contralateral loss of proprioception, vibration, and fine touch. Spinothalamic Tract : Damage results in contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation. Cranial Nerves IX, X, XI, XII : Affected nerves cause dysphagia, dysarthria, tongue deviation, and hoarseness. (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cardiovascular-Respiratory Centers : Disruption causes unstable blood pressure and irregular breathing. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nucleus of the Solitary Tract (NTS) : Impairment leads to altered baroreflex, respiratory issues, and loss of taste."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Area Postrema : Damage causes nausea, vomiting, and reduced gag reflex."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus : Lesion results in ipsilateral facial pain and loss of temperature/pain sensation. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inferior Olivary Nucleus : Affected motor coordination leads to ataxia and tremors."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pyramidal Decussation : Damage causes contralateral muscle weakness or paralysis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cuneate and Gracile Nuclei : Lesion causes loss of proprioception and fine touch on the ipsilateral side."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Medial Lemniscus : Impairment leads to contralateral loss of proprioception, vibration, and fine touch."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spinothalamic Tract : Damage results in contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cranial Nerves IX, X, XI, XII : Affected nerves cause dysphagia, dysarthria, tongue deviation, and hoarseness. (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cardiovascular-Respiratory Centers : Disruption causes unstable blood pressure and irregular breathing. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nucleus of the Solitary Tract (NTS) : Impairment leads to altered baroreflex, respiratory issues, and loss of taste."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Area Postrema : Damage causes nausea, vomiting, and reduced gag reflex."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus : Lesion results in ipsilateral facial pain and loss of temperature/pain sensation. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inferior Olivary Nucleus : Affected motor coordination leads to ataxia and tremors."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pyramidal Decussation : Damage causes contralateral muscle weakness or paralysis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cuneate and Gracile Nuclei : Lesion causes loss of proprioception and fine touch on the ipsilateral side."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Medial Lemniscus : Impairment leads to contralateral loss of proprioception, vibration, and fine touch."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spinothalamic Tract : Damage results in contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cranial Nerves IX, X, XI, XII : Affected nerves cause dysphagia, dysarthria, tongue deviation, and hoarseness. (Option B ruled out)"}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 58-year-old man is admitted to the hospital after suddenly collapsing at home. Upon examination, he is fully conscious and aware of his surroundings but is unable to move any limbs or speak. He shows no signs of respiratory distress but struggles with swallowing and cannot perform any voluntary movements, except for blinking and vertical eye movements. What condition is this patient most likely experiencing?","o":[{"t":"A) Parinaud Syndrome"},{"t":"B) Claude Syndrome"},{"t":"C) Raymond Syndrome"},{"t":"D) Locked-in Syndrome"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient’s condition—complete paralysis of limbs and torso with preserved consciousness and the ability to perform vertical eye movements and blinking—is characteristic of Locked-in Syndrome. This condition results from damage in the brainstem, particularly the anterior pons, affecting motor control while sparing cognitive functions."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Locked-in Syndrome (Pseudocoma)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Locked-in syndrome (LIS) is a condition characterized by quadriplegia, bulbar palsy, and sensory loss due to damage in the brain stem, often the anterior pons."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Causes include vascular lesions, tumors, infections, trauma, and demyelinating disorders."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lesions affect the ventral pons or caudal ventral midbrain while sparing the cerebrum."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Damage to cranial nerve nuclei leads to loss of voluntary muscle control."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Corticobulbar and corticospinal tracts are affected, impacting motor and sensory functions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The reticular activating system, responsible for wakefulness, remains intact."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features: Functions lost: Paralysis of limbs and torso. Bulbar palsy leading to anarthria and dysphagia. Difficulty with breathing, potential apnea, hyperpnea, ataxia. Dizziness, Vertigo Functions retained: Consciousness, orientation and language comprehension. Hearing and ability to perform vertical eye movements and blinking."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Functions lost: Paralysis of limbs and torso. Bulbar palsy leading to anarthria and dysphagia. Difficulty with breathing, potential apnea, hyperpnea, ataxia. Dizziness, Vertigo"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Paralysis of limbs and torso."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bulbar palsy leading to anarthria and dysphagia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Difficulty with breathing, potential apnea, hyperpnea, ataxia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dizziness, Vertigo"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Functions retained: Consciousness, orientation and language comprehension. Hearing and ability to perform vertical eye movements and blinking."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Consciousness, orientation and language comprehension."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hearing and ability to perform vertical eye movements and blinking."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Functions lost: Paralysis of limbs and torso. Bulbar palsy leading to anarthria and dysphagia. Difficulty with breathing, potential apnea, hyperpnea, ataxia. Dizziness, Vertigo"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Paralysis of limbs and torso."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bulbar palsy leading to anarthria and dysphagia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Difficulty with breathing, potential apnea, hyperpnea, ataxia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dizziness, Vertigo"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Functions retained: Consciousness, orientation and language comprehension. Hearing and ability to perform vertical eye movements and blinking."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Consciousness, orientation and language comprehension."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hearing and ability to perform vertical eye movements and blinking."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Paralysis of limbs and torso."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bulbar palsy leading to anarthria and dysphagia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Difficulty with breathing, potential apnea, hyperpnea, ataxia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dizziness, Vertigo"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Consciousness, orientation and language comprehension."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hearing and ability to perform vertical eye movements and blinking."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Parinaud Syndrome (Option A) results from lesions in the midbrain, often affecting the vertical gaze. Patients may have difficulties with eye movement but typically retain motor function and consciousness, which does not match the clinical presentation in the scenario."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Claude Syndrome (Option B) is caused by lesions in the midbrain that affect the oculomotor nerve and the red nucleus, leading to oculomotor palsy and ataxia. Patients may have motor and sensory deficits, but unlike Locked-in Syndrome, they do not retain the ability to perform vertical eye movements."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Raymond Syndrome (Option C) results from lesions affecting the corticospinal and abducens pathways in the pons, leading to contralateral hemiparesis and ipsilateral abducens nerve palsy. While it shares some similarities, it does not present with the preserved consciousness and eye movement found in Locked-in Syndrome."}]},{"n":7,"q":"A 64-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after experiencing sudden weakness on the left side of his body. On examination, he exhibits right-sided facial droop and difficulty moving his right eye laterally. He has also lost the ability to feel pain and temperature on the right side of his body. What type of stroke is this patient most likely experiencing?","o":[{"t":"A) Foville Syndrome"},{"t":"B) Millard-Gubler Syndrome"},{"t":"C) Dysarthria-Clumsy Hand Syndrome"},{"t":"D) Raymond Syndrome"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient's symptoms—contralateral hemiparesis, ipsilateral facial nerve palsy, and ipsilateral abducens palsy—are characteristic of Millard-Gubler Syndrome, which results from a ventro-caudal pontine infarction. This aligns with the specific deficits observed in the clinical scenario."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pontine Stroke Syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ventro-caudal Pontine Infarction Contralateral hemiparesis (corticospinal tract). -Paramedian perforating arteries arising from the Ipsilateral abducens palsy (abducens nerve). basilar artery. Ipsilateral facial nerve palsy (facial nerve)."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation (lateral spinothalamic tract). Millard-Gubler Syndrome : Contralateral hemiparesis, ipsilateral abducens, and facial nerve palsy. Foville Syndrome : Same as Millard-Gubler with additional conjugate gaze palsy. (Option A ruled out) Raymond Syndrome : Contralateral hemiparesis with ipsilateral abducens palsy, no facial nerve involvement. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mid-pontine Base Infarction Ipsilateral ataxia (pontine nuclei). -Paramedian arteries or short circumferential Ipsilateral facial sensorimotor weakness (trigeminal arteries, both arising from the basilar artery. nerve)."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Contralateral hemiparesis (corticospinal tract). Pure Motor Hemiparesis : Isolated contralateral motor weakness due to corticospinal tract infarct. Ataxic Hemiparesis : Ipsilateral ataxia with contralateral motor weakness. Dysarthria-Clumsy Hand Syndrome : Dysarthria, dysphagia, impaired hand dexterity, and weakness. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tegmental Pontine Syndrome Ipsilateral facial sensory disturbance (trigeminal -Rostral Pontine Syndrome: Anterior inferior nucleus). cerebellar artery or superior cerebellar artery. Ipsilateral masticator paralysis (trigeminal motor -Caudal Pontine Syndrome: Anterior inferior nucleus). cerebellar artery or short circumferential arteries. Impaired blinking (tectospinal tract)."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Contralateral hemisensory loss (medial lemniscus and lateral spinothalamic tract). Ipsilateral hemiataxia (superior cerebellar peduncle)."}]},{"n":8,"q":"A 65-year-old man presents with severe burning pain on the left side of his body three months after having a stroke. He describes the pain as constant and stabbing, with episodes of intense shooting pain lasting several minutes. Physical examination reveals hyperalgesia and allodynia in the affected areas, with abnormal temperature sensation. MRI shows a lesion in the ventroposterolateral thalamus. Which of the following best describes this condition?","o":[{"t":"A. Complex regional pain syndrome with peripheral nerve involvement"},{"t":"B. Dejerine Roussy syndrome with central thalamic pain"},{"t":"C. Trigeminal neuralgia with facial pain"},{"t":"D. Post-herpetic neuralgia with dermatomal distribution"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The correct answer is Dejerine Roussy syndrome with central thalamic pain because this case presents with the classic triad of features that define Dejerine Roussy syndrome:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Dejerine Roussy Syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pain Components Spontaneous constant pain (burning, aching, freezing)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Spontaneous intermittent pain (shooting, lancinating) Evoked pain (hyperalgesia, allodynia)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pain Characteristics Sharp, burning, stabbing quality"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Affects areas corresponding to stroke May be immediate or delayed up to several months"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Sensory Findings Abnormal pain/temperature sensation (90% cases)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Hyperalgesia Allodynia Variable touch/vibration changes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Anatomical Location Primarily: Ventroposterolateral thalamus"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Can occur with lesions anywhere along spinothalamic tract"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A is incorrect because complex regional pain syndrome is a peripheral nerve condition typically following trauma or surgery."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C is incorrect because trigeminal neuralgia specifically affects the distribution of the trigeminal nerve with different pain characteristics."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D is incorrect because post-herpetic neuralgia follows herpes zoster infection and has a specific dermatomal distribution."}]},{"n":9,"q":"A 12-year-old boy presents with progressive difficulty looking upward and complains of double vision. On examination, he demonstrates impaired upward gaze, jerky convergence movements of both eyes on attempted upward gaze, and pupils that constrict poorly to light but constrict well with near vision. He has adopted a chin-up position to compensate. MRI reveals a mass in the pineal region. Which of the following best characterizes this condition and its classic triad?","o":[{"t":"A. Paralysis of downward gaze, convergence weakness, and pineal mass"},{"t":"B. Paralysis of upward gaze, convergence-retraction nystagmus, and pupillary light-near dissociation"},{"t":"C. Paralysis of lateral gaze, retraction nystagmus, and normal pupils"},{"t":"D. Complete ophthalmoplegia, dilated pupils, and pineal calcification"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Parinaud Syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Classic Triad 1. Impaired upward gaze (upgaze palsy)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"2. Convergence-retraction nystagmus 3. Pupillary light-near dissociation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Common Symptoms - Difficulty looking up"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Diplopia (65% of patients) - Blurred near vision - Oscillopsia - Compensatory chin-up position"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Additional Signs - Collier's sign (lid retraction) in 40%"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- \"Setting sun\" sign - Loss of convergence (<25% of patients)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Common Causes - Pineal tumors (most common in young)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Midbrain infarction - Hydrocephalus - Multiple sclerosis - Hemorrhage - Infections (toxoplasmosis)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Anatomical Location Dorsal midbrain/pretectal area at level of superior"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"colliculus"}]},{"n":10,"q":"A 62-year-old man presents with a sudden onset of left-sided drooping eyelid and difficulty coordinating movements of his right arm and leg. On examination, he has left-sided ptosis, dilated left pupil, and the left eye is \"down and out.\" He demonstrates significant right-sided ataxia but has normal strength in all extremities. Which of the following conditions represent the suspected diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"A. Weber syndrome"},{"t":"B. Claude syndrome"},{"t":"C. Benedikt syndrome"},{"t":"D. Nothnagel syndrome"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Claude Syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anatomical Location Midbrain (Red Nucleus Region)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Structures Affected - Oculomotor nerve (CN III)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Red nucleus - Dentatothalamic tract"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ipsilateral Findings (CN III) - Ptosis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Dilated pupil - \"Down and out\" eye position - Diplopia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Contralateral Findings - Cerebellar ataxia"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Limb incoordination - No hemiparesis (key distinguishing feature) - Intention tremor"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Key Distinguishing Features - No hemiparesis (unlike Weber)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- No choreiform movements (unlike Benedikt) - Contralateral ataxia (unlike Nothnagel)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Vascular Supply Paramedian branches of posterior cerebral artery"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Common Causes - Stroke (most common)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Hemorrhage - Tumor - Multiple sclerosis"}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 45-year-old woman presents with double vision and difficulty maintaining balance while walking. On examination, she has drooping of the right eyelid, a dilated right pupil, and significant unsteadiness with right-sided limb ataxia. Unlike other midbrain syndromes, she has no weakness in her extremities. Which of the following are the structures affected?","o":[{"t":"A. Superior cerebellar peduncle and CN III"},{"t":"Cerebral peduncle and CN III"},{"t":"C. Red nucleus and CN III"},{"t":"D. Red nucleus and CN III"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient symptoms are suggestive of Nothnagel Syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Nothnagel Syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Anatomical Location Dorsal Midbrain (Quadrigeminal Plate)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Structures Affected - Oculomotor nerve (CN III)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Superior cerebellar peduncle"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ipsilateral Findings - CN III palsy (ptosis, dilated pupil, eye deviation)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Cerebellar ataxia - Limb incoordination - Gait disturbance"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Key Distinguishing Features - No contralateral hemiparesis (unlike Weber)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- No tremors (unlike Benedikt) - Often bilateral - Commonly associated with tumors"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Common Causes - Pineal region tumors"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Quadrigeminal plate tumors - Vascular lesions - Multiple sclerosis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Significance - Often presents as neuro-ophthalmological emergency"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- May be first sign of pineal region tumor - Usually requires neuroimaging for diagnosis"}]},{"n":12,"q":"A 58-year-old man presents with a sudden onset of right-sided weakness and left-sided drooping of the eyelid. On examination, he has left-sided ptosis, a dilated left pupil that doesn't react to light, and the left eye is \"down and out.\" He also has right-sided hemiparesis affecting his face, arm, and leg. Which of the following best describes this syndrome and its anatomical location?","o":[{"t":"A. Claude syndrome - Red nucleus and CN III"},{"t":"B. Weber syndrome - Cerebral peduncle and CN III"},{"t":"C. Benedict syndrome - Red nucleus and superior cerebellar peduncle"},{"t":"D. Millard-Gubler syndrome - Ventral pons affecting CN VI and VII"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Weber Syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Characteristic Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Anatomical Location Ventral Midbrain (Cerebral Peduncle)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Structures Affected - Oculomotor nerve (CN III)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Cerebral peduncle (corticospinal tract)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ipsilateral Findings (CN III) - Ptosis (drooping eyelid)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Dilated pupil (parasympathetic) - \"Down and out\" eye position - Impaired medial, upward, and downward gaze"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Contralateral Findings - Hemiparesis/hemiplegia (face, arm, leg)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Spastic paralysis - Hyperreflexia - Babinski sign"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Vascular Supply Paramedian branches of the posterior cerebral artery"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Common Causes - Stroke (most common)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Tumor - Hemorrhage - Multiple sclerosis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Claude syndrome - Red nucleus and CN III (Option A): Involves the red nucleus and CN III, causing oculomotor nerve palsy with contralateral ataxia rather than hemiparesis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Benedict syndrome - Red nucleus and superior cerebellar peduncle (Option C): Affects the red nucleus and superior cerebellar peduncle, leading to oculomotor nerve palsy along with contralateral ataxia and tremor, not pure hemiparesis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Millard-Gubler syndrome - Ventral pons affecting CN VI and VII (Option D): Involves the ventral pons with CN VI and VII, leading to contralateral hemiparesis but does not affect CN III or cause a \"down and out\" eye."}]},{"n":13,"q":"A 54-year-old male presents with a sudden onset of double vision, left-sided weakness, and difficulty moving his right eye. On examination, the patient has a right-sided ptosis, a dilated pupil that does not react to light, and an outward deviation of the right eye. The left side of his body shows weakness with hyperreflexia. MRI reveals an ischemic lesion in the midbrain. Which of the following structures is most likely involved in this patient’s condition?","o":[{"t":"A) Trochlear nerve nucleus and Crus cerebri"},{"t":"B) Oculomotor nerve nucleus and Crus cerebri"},{"t":"C) Substantia nigra and Crus cerebri"},{"t":"D) Crus cerebri only"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This patient’s presentation is indicative of Weber syndrome(Medial midbrain syndrome) , which involves a lesion in the midbrain affecting both the oculomotor nerve and the corticospinal tract in the crus cerebri. Arteries involved are paramedian branches of the posterior cerebral artery."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Structures Involved in a Midbrain Stroke:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Function: Controls most of the eye movements, pupillary constriction, and eyelid elevation (levator palpebrae superioris)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical Findings: Damage to the oculomotor nerve leads to ptosis , a dilated pupil (mydriasis) , and an eye that is deviated outward and downward (due to unopposed action of the lateral rectus and superior oblique muscles)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Function: Innervates the superior oblique muscle, which helps in depression and intorsion of the eye."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical Findings: A lesion of the trochlear nerve causes vertical diplopia - difficulty looking downwards and inwards, noticed while reading or descending stairs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Trochlear nerve damage would not explain the ptosis or mydriasis seen in this patient (Option A ruled out) ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Function: Part of the basal ganglia, it plays a crucial role in motor control and coordination, with loss of dopamine-producing neurons here leading to Parkinsonism ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical Findings: Midbrain strokes affecting the substantia nigra can result in motor deficits, tremors, and bradykinesia, similar to Parkinson's disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's main issues involve cranial nerve III and motor weakness, not Parkinsonism features (Option C ruled out)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Function: Contains descending fibers of the corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts, responsible for voluntary motor control."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical Findings: Damage here leads to contralateral hemiparesis due to involvement of the corticospinal tract. In Weber syndrome, the lesion commonly affects both the oculomotor nerve and the crus cerebri."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While the crus cerebri is affected in this case, it alone does not account for the cranial nerve signs (Option D ruled out)."}]},{"n":14,"q":"A 65-year-old male presents with sudden onset right-sided weakness, inability to speak, and unawareness of his surroundings. On examination, he has complete right-sided hemiplegia, and global aphasia, and is neglecting the right side of his visual field. Non-contrast CT of the brain shows a hypodense area involving the left middle cerebral artery territory. What is the most likely type of stroke?","o":[{"t":"A. Lacunar Stroke"},{"t":"B. Posterior Circulation Stroke"},{"t":"C. Partial Anterior Circulation Stroke"},{"t":"D. Total Anterior Circulation Stroke"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Total Anterior Circulation Stroke (TACS) is one of the most severe types of ischemic strokes involving large vessels, usually the internal carotid artery or the proximal middle cerebral artery (MCA) . TACS presents with a triad of features:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This patient has all three cardinal features of TACS, making it the most likely diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lacunar Stroke (Option A) :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Involves small perforating arteries and affects the subcortical structures such as the basal ganglia, internal capsule, thalamus, and pons."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lacunar strokes do not involve cortical symptoms like aphasia or visual field defects, as seen in this patient."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"They present with \"pure motor,\" \"pure sensory,\" or ataxic hemiparesis syndromes, but cortical signs are absent, ruling this out."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Posterior Circulation Stroke (Option B) :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Strokes in the posterior circulation involve the vertebrobasilar system, including the brainstem, cerebellum, and occipital lobes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms typically include dizziness, ataxia, visual disturbances like cortical blindness, and cranial nerve abnormalities, which do not match this patient’s presentation of hemiplegia, aphasia, and neglect."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Partial Anterior Circulation Stroke (PACS) (Option C) :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PACS affects only part of the anterior circulation, usually just the MCA or ACA territory."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PACS is differentiated from TACS by the fact that not all three key features (hemiplegia, cortical dysfunction, and visual field defect) are present."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In PACS, one or two features of TACS may be seen, but the full triad is absent."}]},{"n":15,"q":"A 68-year-old man presents with a sudden onset of right-sided weakness and aphasia. CT angiography reveals an occlusion in one of the arteries. Which of the following best describes the location of this occlusion?","o":[{"t":"B- M1 Segment of MCA"},{"t":"B. D- M1 Segment of MCA"},{"t":"B- M2 Segment of MCA"},{"t":"D. D- ACA"}],"a":[0,1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The middle cerebral artery (MCA) is divided into four segments: M1, M2, M3, and M4. The M1 segment, also known as the horizontal or sphenoidal segment , is the proximal portion of the MCA that extends from its origin at the internal carotid artery bifurcation to its bifurcation or trifurcation into the M2 segments."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"An occlusion in this segment would likely result in the symptoms described (right-sided weakness and aphasia), as it supplies a large portion of the lateral cerebral cortex, including motor and language areas ."},{"k":"img","v":7501},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Internal Carotid Artery, Anterior Cerebral Artery, Middle Cerebral Artery"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Vessel Location/Course Key Areas Supplied Clinical Features When"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Occluded"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Internal Carotid Ascends through neck, Gives rise to MCA and Large territory infarcts Artery (ICA) enters skull via carotid ACA affecting both MCA and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"canal ACA distributions"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anterior Cerebral Courses anteriorly and Medial surface of cerebral Contralateral leg Artery (ACA) curves around corpus hemisphere, anterior 3/4 weakness, behavioral (Option D) callosum of corpus callosum changes"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"MCA - M1 Horizontal course from Lenticulostriate arteries to Contralateral hemiparesis, Segment ICA bifurcation to MCA basal ganglia aphasia (if dominant"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"bifurcation hemisphere)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"MCA - M2 Insular segment in Insular cortex and More restricted deficits Segment (Option Sylvian fissure operculum than M1 occlusion C)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"MCA - M3 Opercular segment Lateral convexity of Specific cortical deficits Segment hemisphere based on location"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"MCA - M4 Cortical branches over Specific cortical regions Focal deficits based on Segment lateral surface affected region"}],"qi":[7463]},{"n":16,"q":"A 62-year-old male presents with sudden onset right-sided weakness, slurred speech, and difficulty comprehending language. A CT scan reveals an ischemic stroke. The stroke likely involves which of the following vessels?","o":[{"t":"A. Anterior cerebral artery (ACA)"},{"t":"B. Middle cerebral artery (MCA)"},{"t":"C. Posterior cerebral artery (PCA)"},{"t":"D. Basilar artery"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Blood Supply of the Brain:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The brain is primarily supplied by two systems: the anterior circulation from the internal carotid arteries and the posterior circulation from the vertebral arteries . These arteries anastomose to form the Circle of Willis , providing collateral circulation between the anterior and posterior blood supplies"},{"k":"img","v":7507},{"k":"img","v":7509},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1. Internal Carotid Artery (ICA)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Branches : Ophthalmic artery : Supplies the eye and orbital structures. Posterior communicating artery (PCoA) : Connects the internal carotid artery with the posterior cerebral artery. Anterior cerebral artery (ACA) : Supplies medial portions of the frontal and parietal lobes. Middle cerebral artery (MCA) : The largest branch, supplies the lateral aspect of the brain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ophthalmic artery : Supplies the eye and orbital structures."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Posterior communicating artery (PCoA) : Connects the internal carotid artery with the posterior cerebral artery."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anterior cerebral artery (ACA) : Supplies medial portions of the frontal and parietal lobes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Middle cerebral artery (MCA) : The largest branch, supplies the lateral aspect of the brain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Internal carotid artery occlusion can cause ischemia in MCA or ACA territory or a watershed (border zone) infarct between these areas."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ophthalmic artery : Supplies the eye and orbital structures."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Posterior communicating artery (PCoA) : Connects the internal carotid artery with the posterior cerebral artery."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anterior cerebral artery (ACA) : Supplies medial portions of the frontal and parietal lobes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Middle cerebral artery (MCA) : The largest branch, supplies the lateral aspect of the brain."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2. Anterior Cerebral Artery (ACA)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Supply : Medial portions of the frontal and parietal lobes, including the superior portion of the motor and sensory cortices (leg area)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ACA Stroke : (Option A) Contralateral weakness and sensory loss, primarily affecting the lower limb (leg > arm). Frontal lobe symptoms, including abulia (lack of initiative) and personality changes. Urinary incontinence due to damage to the medial frontal lobes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Contralateral weakness and sensory loss, primarily affecting the lower limb (leg > arm)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Frontal lobe symptoms, including abulia (lack of initiative) and personality changes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Urinary incontinence due to damage to the medial frontal lobes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Contralateral weakness and sensory loss, primarily affecting the lower limb (leg > arm)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Frontal lobe symptoms, including abulia (lack of initiative) and personality changes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Urinary incontinence due to damage to the medial frontal lobes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"3. Middle Cerebral Artery (MCA)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Supply : Lateral surface of the frontal, parietal, and temporal lobes; basal ganglia; and internal capsule."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MCA Stroke : (Option B) Contralateral hemiparesis and hemisensory loss (face and arm > leg). Aphasia if the dominant hemisphere (usually left) is affected. Hemineglect if the non-dominant hemisphere is involved. Homonymous hemianopia due to optic radiation involvement."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Contralateral hemiparesis and hemisensory loss (face and arm > leg)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aphasia if the dominant hemisphere (usually left) is affected."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemineglect if the non-dominant hemisphere is involved."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Homonymous hemianopia due to optic radiation involvement."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Contralateral hemiparesis and hemisensory loss (face and arm > leg)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aphasia if the dominant hemisphere (usually left) is affected."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemineglect if the non-dominant hemisphere is involved."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Homonymous hemianopia due to optic radiation involvement."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"4. Posterior Cerebral Artery (PCA)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Supply : Occipital lobe (visual cortex), inferior temporal lobe, thalamus, and parts of the midbrain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PCA Stroke : (Option C) Contralateral homonymous hemianopia (loss of the same side of the visual field in both eyes). Thalamic pain syndrome (Dejerine-Roussy syndrome) if the thalamus is involved, leading to contralateral sensory loss followed by chronic pain. Memory disturbances if the temporal lobe is affected."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Contralateral homonymous hemianopia (loss of the same side of the visual field in both eyes)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thalamic pain syndrome (Dejerine-Roussy syndrome) if the thalamus is involved, leading to contralateral sensory loss followed by chronic pain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Memory disturbances if the temporal lobe is affected."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Contralateral homonymous hemianopia (loss of the same side of the visual field in both eyes)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thalamic pain syndrome (Dejerine-Roussy syndrome) if the thalamus is involved, leading to contralateral sensory loss followed by chronic pain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Memory disturbances if the temporal lobe is affected."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"5. Vertebral Arteries"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Branches : Posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA) : Supplies the cerebellum and lateral medulla. PICA Stroke : Wallenberg syndrome (lateral medullary syndrome): Causes ipsilateral facial numbness, contralateral body numbness, ataxia, vertigo, and dysphagia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA) : Supplies the cerebellum and lateral medulla."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PICA Stroke : Wallenberg syndrome (lateral medullary syndrome): Causes ipsilateral facial numbness, contralateral body numbness, ataxia, vertigo, and dysphagia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA) : Supplies the cerebellum and lateral medulla."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PICA Stroke : Wallenberg syndrome (lateral medullary syndrome): Causes ipsilateral facial numbness, contralateral body numbness, ataxia, vertigo, and dysphagia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"6. Basilar Artery"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Supply : Pons, midbrain, occipital lobes, and cerebellum."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Branches : Anterior inferior cerebellar artery (AICA) : Supplies the cerebellum and parts of the brainstem. Superior cerebellar artery (SCA) : Supplies the superior cerebellum and parts of the midbrain. Posterior cerebral arteries (PCA) : Terminal branches that supply the occipital lobe."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anterior inferior cerebellar artery (AICA) : Supplies the cerebellum and parts of the brainstem."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Superior cerebellar artery (SCA) : Supplies the superior cerebellum and parts of the midbrain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Posterior cerebral arteries (PCA) : Terminal branches that supply the occipital lobe."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Basilar Artery Stroke : (Option D) Locked-in syndrome : Quadriplegia with preserved consciousness and vertical eye movements due to damage to the pons. Cranial nerve palsies (especially those involving eye movements like the oculomotor nerve). Ataxia , vertigo, and dysarthria due to cerebellar involvement."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Locked-in syndrome : Quadriplegia with preserved consciousness and vertical eye movements due to damage to the pons."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cranial nerve palsies (especially those involving eye movements like the oculomotor nerve)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ataxia , vertigo, and dysarthria due to cerebellar involvement."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anterior inferior cerebellar artery (AICA) : Supplies the cerebellum and parts of the brainstem."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Superior cerebellar artery (SCA) : Supplies the superior cerebellum and parts of the midbrain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Posterior cerebral arteries (PCA) : Terminal branches that supply the occipital lobe."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Locked-in syndrome : Quadriplegia with preserved consciousness and vertical eye movements due to damage to the pons."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cranial nerve palsies (especially those involving eye movements like the oculomotor nerve)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ataxia , vertigo, and dysarthria due to cerebellar involvement."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"7. Anterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery (AICA)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Supply : Cerebellum, lower pons."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"AICA Stroke : Ipsilateral ataxia, vertigo, hearing loss (due to involvement of the inner ear via the labyrinthine artery), facial weakness, and contralateral sensory loss."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ipsilateral ataxia, vertigo, hearing loss (due to involvement of the inner ear via the labyrinthine artery), facial weakness, and contralateral sensory loss."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ipsilateral ataxia, vertigo, hearing loss (due to involvement of the inner ear via the labyrinthine artery), facial weakness, and contralateral sensory loss."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"8. Superior Cerebellar Artery (SCA)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Supply : Superior part of the cerebellum and parts of the midbrain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"SCA Stroke : Ipsilateral cerebellar ataxia, nausea, vomiting, and dysarthria."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ipsilateral cerebellar ataxia, nausea, vomiting, and dysarthria."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ipsilateral cerebellar ataxia, nausea, vomiting, and dysarthria."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"9. Anterior Spinal Artery"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Supply : Anterior two-thirds of the spinal cord and parts of the medulla."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anterior Spinal Artery Syndrome : Medial medullary syndrome : Results in contralateral hemiparesis, loss of proprioception, and ipsilateral hypoglossal nerve dysfunction (tongue deviation)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Medial medullary syndrome : Results in contralateral hemiparesis, loss of proprioception, and ipsilateral hypoglossal nerve dysfunction (tongue deviation)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Medial medullary syndrome : Results in contralateral hemiparesis, loss of proprioception, and ipsilateral hypoglossal nerve dysfunction (tongue deviation)."}]},{"n":17,"q":"A 56-year-old man with a history of hypertension and hyperlipidemia is brought to the emergency department after suddenly losing vision in the left visual field of both eyes. He denies any headache, weakness, or sensory deficits. On examination, his visual acuity is intact, but he has left homonymous hemianopia. There is no evidence of motor or sensory loss. Where is the most likely site of the stroke?","o":[{"t":"A) Right Occipital Lobe"},{"t":"B) Left Occipital Lobe"},{"t":"C) Right Parietal Lobe"},{"t":"D) Left Parietal Lobe"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The occipital lobe is involved in the analysis of vision and processing of visual stimuli. The visual pathway involves the optic nerves, chiasm, and radiations that project to the occipital lobe."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The primary visual cortex in this lobe receives and interprets visual information from the contralateral visual field, meaning a lesion in the right occipital lobe will affect the left visual field of both eyes and vice versa."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this patient, the presence of left homonymous hemianopia suggests damage to the visual pathways beyond the optic chiasm, specifically affecting the right occipital lobe."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Visual field loss that affects the same side of both eyes (homonymous hemianopia) is a hallmark of occipital lobe stroke, contralateral to the side of the visual field defect ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Left homonymous hemianopia in Right occipital lobe disease"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Localization of Stroke in the Occipital Lobe:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Effects of Unilateral Occipital Lobe Disease (Right or Left):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Contralateral homonymous hemianopia : This is a characteristic feature of an occipital lobe lesion. The visual field loss is congruent , meaning it affects the same portion of the visual field in both eyes. It may involve the central field ( splitting the macula ) or peripheral fields."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Homonymous hemiachromatopsia : Loss of color vision in the same half of the visual field."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It may involve the central field ( splitting the macula ) or peripheral fields."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Homonymous hemiachromatopsia : Loss of color vision in the same half of the visual field."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Irritative lesions can lead to elementary visual hallucinations , such as flashes of light or geometric shapes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It may involve the central field ( splitting the macula ) or peripheral fields."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Homonymous hemiachromatopsia : Loss of color vision in the same half of the visual field."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Effects of Right Occipital Lobe Disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Visual illusions and hallucinations : More extensive lesions in the right occipital lobe may produce complex visual disturbances , such as visual illusions (distorted perception of objects) and hallucinations (seeing things that are not present)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Loss of topographic memory and visual orientation : Patients may have difficulty recognizing familiar places or navigating through space, affecting their ability to understand spatial relationships."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Effects of Left Occipital Lobe Disease:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In addition to the visual field deficits described above,"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lesion of the splenium of the corpus callosum : This can cause alexia without agraphia , where the patient loses the ability to read while maintaining the ability to write."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Object agnosia : The inability to recognize or identify objects despite having normal vision."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Effects of Bilateral Occipital Lobe Disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cortical blindness : The patient is unable to see, but the pupils remain reactive to light."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anton’s syndrome : A rare condition where the patient is unaware of their blindness and may confabulate about their visual experiences."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prosopagnosia : The inability to recognize familiar faces, even though basic vision is preserved."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Simultanagnosia : The inability to synthesize multiple elements of a visual scene, making it difficult to read more than one word or letter at a time."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Balint’s syndrome : A combination of symptoms affecting visual attention and coordination, including: Inability to voluntarily look into the peripheral field, despite full eye movements. Difficulty in reaching for objects under visual guidance. Visual inattention affecting mainly the peripheral visual field."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inability to voluntarily look into the peripheral field, despite full eye movements."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Difficulty in reaching for objects under visual guidance."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Visual inattention affecting mainly the peripheral visual field."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inability to voluntarily look into the peripheral field, despite full eye movements."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Difficulty in reaching for objects under visual guidance."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Visual inattention affecting mainly the peripheral visual field."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Left Occipital Lobe (Option B) : A stroke in the left occipital lobe would result in right homonymous hemianopia , not the left-sided visual field loss seen in this patient."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Right Parietal Lobe (Option C) : The parietal lobe is primarily involved in sensory processing and spatial awareness. Lesions in this area can cause neglect or sensory deficits, but they do not typically cause isolated visual field defects like homonymous hemianopia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Left Parietal Lobe (Option D) : Similarly, a stroke in the left parietal lobe would result in sensory or language deficits (if it’s the dominant hemisphere), but would not produce the homonymous hemianopia seen in this case."}]},{"n":18,"q":"A 70-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after her family noticed significant changes in her behavior and difficulty speaking. Upon examination, she displays weakness on the right side of her body and struggles to form coherent sentences, often speaking in short, fragmented phrases. The family also reports that she has become more irritable and has difficulty managing her daily tasks. What type of stroke is this patient most likely experiencing?","o":[{"t":"A) Right Temporal Lobe Stroke"},{"t":"B) Frontal Lobe Stroke"},{"t":"C) Occipital Lobe Stroke"},{"t":"D) Left Parietal Lobe Stroke"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient’s symptoms—right-sided weakness (hemiparesis), difficulty with speech formation (indicative of Broca’s aphasia), and behavioral changes such as irritability—are characteristic of a frontal lobe stroke."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The frontal lobe is crucial for language, executive function, and emotional regulation, which aligns with the impairments observed in this case."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Frontal Lobe Stroke:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Obstruction of the anterior cerebral artery (ACA)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Motor impairments: Hemiparesis/Hemiplegia: Weakness or paralysis on the opposite side of the body. Ataxia: Uncoordinated movement, affecting limbs, gait, speech, and swallowing. Dysphagia: Difficulty swallowing."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemiparesis/Hemiplegia: Weakness or paralysis on the opposite side of the body."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ataxia: Uncoordinated movement, affecting limbs, gait, speech, and swallowing."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dysphagia: Difficulty swallowing."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Speech and language: Aphasia: Especially common in left frontal lobe stroke (Broca’s area), affecting speech and language formation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aphasia: Especially common in left frontal lobe stroke (Broca’s area), affecting speech and language formation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cognitive deficits: Executive dysfunction: Impaired decision-making, planning, attention, and multitasking. Vascular dementia: Memory loss, poor impulse control, and behavioral changes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Executive dysfunction: Impaired decision-making, planning, attention, and multitasking."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vascular dementia: Memory loss, poor impulse control, and behavioral changes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Behavior and personality changes: Emotional dysregulation: Irritability, impulsiveness, apathy, anxiety, or decreased motivation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Emotional dysregulation: Irritability, impulsiveness, apathy, anxiety, or decreased motivation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Incontinence: Loss of bladder and bowel control."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Impaired spatial reasoning: Difficulty with spatial awareness and navigation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemiparesis/Hemiplegia: Weakness or paralysis on the opposite side of the body."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ataxia: Uncoordinated movement, affecting limbs, gait, speech, and swallowing."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dysphagia: Difficulty swallowing."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aphasia: Especially common in left frontal lobe stroke (Broca’s area), affecting speech and language formation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Executive dysfunction: Impaired decision-making, planning, attention, and multitasking."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vascular dementia: Memory loss, poor impulse control, and behavioral changes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Emotional dysregulation: Irritability, impulsiveness, apathy, anxiety, or decreased motivation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Each hemisphere affects the opposite side of the body, with left frontal lobe strokes affecting speech more, and right frontal lobe strokes often leading to spatial reasoning impairments."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Right Temporal Lobe Stroke (Option A) affects auditory processing and memory, leading to difficulties with sound recognition and visual memory, but it would not typically cause significant speech impairments or changes in behavior."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Occipital Lobe Stroke (Option C) primarily results in visual disturbances, such as blurred vision or visual field loss, and would not account for the cognitive and speech issues present in this scenario."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Left Parietal Lobe Stroke (Option D) can cause issues with language and spatial awareness but does not typically lead to the emotional and executive function deficits observed in this patient."}]},{"n":19,"q":"A 55-year-old right-handed man presents to the emergency department after his partner noticed he has been struggling to communicate effectively. He speaks in long sentences but often uses nonsensical words and seems unaware of his errors. Additionally, he has difficulty remembering recent conversations and frequently confuses familiar faces. What type of stroke is this patient most likely experiencing?","o":[{"t":"A) Right Temporal Lobe Stroke"},{"t":"B) Left Temporal Lobe Stroke"},{"t":"C) Right Parietal Lobe Stroke"},{"t":"D) Frontal Lobe Stroke"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The left temporal lobe is primarily responsible for language processing and verbal memory, making it the most likely site of the stroke in this case."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's symptoms—fluent speech with nonsensical word use (indicative of Wernicke’s aphasia), difficulty recalling recent conversations, and trouble recognizing faces—are characteristic of a left temporal lobe stroke."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Features of Left Temporal Lobe Stroke Features of Right Temporal Lobe Stroke"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Impaired language and memory: Difficulty with spoken Poor visual memory and spatial awareness: Difficulty language and remembering verbal information. recognizing visual patterns and spatial information. Fluent (Wernicke’s) aphasia: Fluent speech with Prosopagnosia : Inability to recognize faces, incorrect words, affecting language comprehension. including familiar ones. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"General Features of Temporal Lobe Stroke"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Poor memory: Difficulty recalling past memories and learning new information. Prosopagnosia: Trouble recognizing faces and objects. Fluent aphasia: Specifically in left-sided strokes, affecting speech fluency but with incorrect word use. Visual perception difficulties: Challenges with interpreting the visual field and surroundings. Trouble processing sound: Inability to differentiate or process sounds correctly. Emotional and behavioral changes: Varying behaviors due to damage to the amygdala, such as aggression, passiveness, or difficulty interpreting facial cues."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Right Parietal Lobe Stroke (Option C) usually results in spatial awareness issues, such as hemineglect, poor navigation skills, and difficulty recognizing the left side of the body or surroundings. The symptoms presented in the scenario—such as fluent speech with incorrect word use and memory difficulties—are not consistent with right parietal lobe dysfunction."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Frontal Lobe Stroke (Option D) often leads to changes in personality, impaired executive function, and difficulties with movement (e.g., weakness or apraxia)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While a frontal lobe stroke can affect speech (particularly expressive aphasia), it does not typically result in fluent aphasia or nonsensical speech as described in the scenario. The patient’s symptoms align more closely with a left temporal lobe stroke."}]},{"n":20,"q":"A 62-year-old right-handed woman is brought to the hospital after her husband noticed she was having trouble writing and appeared confused about her left and right sides. Upon examination, she struggles to recognize familiar objects and is unable to perform simple calculations. Her speech is intact, but she has difficulty repeating phrases. What type of stroke is this patient most likely experiencing?","o":[{"t":"A) Right Temporal Lobe Stroke"},{"t":"B) Left Temporal Lobe Stroke"},{"t":"C) Left Parietal Lobe Stroke"},{"t":"D) Right Parietal Lobe Stroke"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient exhibits classic features of a left parietal lobe stroke, including agraphia, right-left confusion, and agnosia. As the dominant hemisphere for a right-handed individual, the left parietal lobe is associated with language processing and numerical abilities, which aligns with her symptoms."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Features of Left Parietal Lobe Stroke Features of Right Parietal Lobe Stroke (Dominant) (Non-Dominant)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Agnosia : Difficulty recognizing objects, Hemineglect : Decreased awareness of the left side of people, or sounds. the body or surroundings. (Option D ruled out) Right-left confusion : Poor differentiation Poor spatial awareness : Difficulty navigating due to between right and left sides. spatial perception issues. Agraphia : Difficulty with writing due to motor Impaired sense of direction and visual memory : or memory issues. Difficulty navigating environments. Acalculia : Inability to process numbers and Anosognosia : Inability to perceive or acknowledge perform simple math. stroke-related deficits. Aphasia : Difficulty with speech, often Proprioception dysfunction : Difficulty sensing body conduction aphasia (speech repetition issues). parts in space, affecting balance and coordination. Gerstmann’s Syndrome : Both lobes can also be affected, leading to further Involves Angular gyrus - Brodmann area 39 memory changes or Balint’s Syndrome (visual Triad of agnosia, agraphia, and acalculia. processing and gaze issues)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Right Temporal Lobe Stroke (Option A) primarily affects auditory processing and memory. Patients may experience difficulties with recognizing familiar sounds or faces and may have issues with visual memory. The symptoms described in the scenario, such as writing difficulties and right-left confusion, do not align with a right temporal lobe stroke."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Left Temporal Lobe Stroke (Option B) can result in language deficits, including difficulty with understanding spoken language and expressive aphasia. While the patient has some speech difficulties, the presence of agnosia, agraphia, and right-left confusion points more specifically to left parietal lobe involvement rather than isolated temporal lobe damage."}]},{"n":21,"q":"A 67-year-old male presents to the emergency department with sudden right-sided weakness and difficulty coordinating his movements. He also reports an altered sensation on the right side. His medical history includes hypertension and diabetes. Upon examination, he shows reduced strength in his right arm and leg. What type of stroke is this patient most likely experiencing?","o":[{"t":"A) Frontal Lobe Stroke"},{"t":"B) Temporal Lobe Stroke"},{"t":"Occipital Lobe Stroke"},{"t":"D) Lacunar Stroke"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This patient’s symptoms—right-sided weakness, coordination difficulties, and altered sensation—are characteristic of a lacunar stroke, which affects subcortical structures."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presentation aligns with pure motor hemiparesis or ataxic hemiparesis, commonly seen in lacunar strokes, especially given his background of hypertension and diabetes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cortical strokes:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Affects the cerebral cortical areas (Lobes)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms include aphasia, neglect, and hemianopia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cortical strokes often begin suddenly and worsen over time."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Frontal Lobe Stroke: Impaired memory, speech difficulties, personality changes, movement issues. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Temporal Lobe Stroke: Short-term memory loss, hearing problems, aphasia (communication disorder). (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Parietal Lobe Stroke: Paresthesia (numbness and tingling), aphasia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Occipital Lobe Stroke: Visual changes, such as blurriness, visual hallucinations, or cortical blindness. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Brainstem strokes:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Affect the brainstem areas- midbrain, pons, and medulla."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms include muscle weakness, hearing and vision problems, sensory changes, balance problems, and difficulty breathing, chewing, swallowing, and speaking."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lacunar strokes:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Affect the subcortical deep brain structures, including the thalamus, basal ganglia, pons, and white matter of the internal capsule."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms include weakness and clumsiness on one side of the body, impaired coordination on one side of the body, and changes in sensations like temperature, touch, or taste."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lacunar stroke presents in Pure motor hemiparesis, Ataxic-hemiparesis, Pure sensory, Dysarthria-clumsy hand and Sensory-motor"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lacunar strokes often worsen in small steps over the initial hours."}]},{"n":22,"q":"A 72-year-old male presents to the emergency department with sudden-onset right-sided weakness and difficulty speaking which started 2 hours ago. His medical history includes hypertension, hyperlipidemia, and a prior TIA. His BP is 180/110 mm Hg. A CT scan of the head rules out hemorrhagic stroke. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management for this patient?","o":[{"t":"A) Administer intravenous Alteplase immediately."},{"t":"B) Start intravenous heparin for anticoagulation therapy."},{"t":"C) Initiate blood pressure management to lower the BP to <160/90 mmHg before considering thrombolytic therapy."},{"t":"D) Begin treatment with antipyretics and surface cooling to manage fever."}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis of ischemic stroke is indicated by the patient's sudden-onset right-sided weakness and difficulty speaking. The CT scan ruling out hemorrhagic stroke further confirms the diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Administering intravenous Alteplase immediately is appropriate as the patient presents with signs of an ischemic stroke and is within the treatment window for intravenous thrombolysis (within 3-4.5 hours). Since the CT scan has ruled out hemorrhagic stroke, administering rtPA is crucial for improving outcomes by restoring blood flow to the affected area of the brain."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management of Ischemic Stroke"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Goal: Preserve the ischemic penumbra."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blood Pressure (BP) Management: Reduce BP if >220/120 mmHg, or >185/110 mmHg if thrombolytic therapy is anticipated. Avoid routine BP lowering to prevent worsening outcomes. Malignant hypertension or myocardial ischemia: Consider lowering heart rate using b1-adrenergic blockers (e.g., esmolol )."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduce BP if >220/120 mmHg, or >185/110 mmHg if thrombolytic therapy is anticipated. Avoid routine BP lowering to prevent worsening outcomes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Malignant hypertension or myocardial ischemia: Consider lowering heart rate using b 1-adrenergic blockers (e.g., esmolol )."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduce BP if >220/120 mmHg, or >185/110 mmHg if thrombolytic therapy is anticipated. Avoid routine BP lowering to prevent worsening outcomes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Malignant hypertension or myocardial ischemia: Consider lowering heart rate using b 1-adrenergic blockers (e.g., esmolol )."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Deep Venous Thrombosis (DVT): Subcutaneous heparin (unfractionated or low-molecular-weight) Pneumatic compression stockings"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Subcutaneous heparin (unfractionated or low-molecular-weight)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pneumatic compression stockings"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cerebral Edema: Peak edema: 2nd-3rd day post-stroke. Mannitol and water restriction can raise serum osmolarity. Avoid hypovolemia Hemicraniectomy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peak edema: 2nd-3rd day post-stroke."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mannitol and water restriction can raise serum osmolarity."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Avoid hypovolemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemicraniectomy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Subcutaneous heparin (unfractionated or low-molecular-weight)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pneumatic compression stockings"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peak edema: 2nd-3rd day post-stroke."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mannitol and water restriction can raise serum osmolarity."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Avoid hypovolemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemicraniectomy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fever is detrimental: Treat with antipyretics and surface cooling."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum glucose management: Keep levels <10.0 mmol/L (180 mg/dL) and >3.3 mmol/L (60 mg/dL)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"IV rtPA (Alteplase) within 3 hours (0.9 mg/kg, max 90 mg, 10% bolus, rest over 60 minutes) is effective despite a 6.4% risk of intracranial hemorrhage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Extended window: 3-4.5 hours (limited efficacy) per ECASS III trial."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tenecteplase (0.25 mg/kg IV bolus) is also used, with simpler administration."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"For large-vessel occlusions (MCA, ICA, basilar artery), mechanical thrombectomy improves outcomes, especially within 6 hours."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aspirin (160-300 mg daily) within 48 hours reduces stroke recurrence."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anticoagulation (heparin, oral anticoagulants) is not recommended for atherothrombotic strokes but may benefit dural sinus thrombosis ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Watch for signs of increased intracranial pressure (ICP) , which can cause brainstem compression."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Suboccipital decompression is recommended before brainstem compression in deteriorating patients."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Early physical, occupational, and speech therapy ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Use of pneumatic compression stockings to prevent DVT."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Constrained movement therapy for hemiparesis, even years post-stroke."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Controversial role of SSRIs in motor recovery but helpful in preventing poststroke depression."},{"k":"img","v":7526}]},{"n":23,"q":"A 65-year-old diabetic male presents to the emergency department with a sudden onset of right-sided weakness and slurred speech that resolved completely within 2 hours. His blood pressure is 160/90 mmHg. Based on his ABCD2 score of 6, what is the most appropriate immediate management?","o":[{"t":"Start oral aspirin 81 mg daily alone"},{"t":"B) Combination of clopidogrel plus aspirin"},{"t":"C) Start warfarin with target INR 2-3"},{"t":"D) Start ticagrelor alone"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Short-term dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT) with clopidogrel-aspirin for 21-90 days is superior to aspirin alone in preventing recurrent stroke after TIA."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This regimen reduces 90-day stroke risk from 11.7% to 8.2% without significant increase in major hemorrhage"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Initial loading dose of clopidogrel 300 mg followed by 75 mg daily with aspirin 75 mg is the recommended protocol"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"ABCD2 score helps stratify TIA patients' risk of stroke: High ABCD2 score (‡4) indicates high risk of early recurrent stroke:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Early aggressive antiplatelet therapy with DAPT (first 21-90 days) followed by monotherapy is the current standard of care for high-risk TIA patients"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Choice of long-term antiplatelet agent (after initial DAPT period) depends on individual patient factors and can include aspirin, clopidogrel, or aspirin-dipyridamole combination."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Aspirin alone (Option A): While aspirin is effective for long-term secondary prevention, using it alone in the acute phase after high-risk TIA (ABCD2 score ‡4) provides suboptimal protection"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Warfarin (Option C): Anticoagulation with warfarin is not indicated for non-cardioembolic TIAs ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is primarily used for cardioembolic stroke prevention in conditions like atrial fibrillation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Has higher bleeding risk compared to antiplatelet therapy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ticagrelor alone (Option D): While ticagrelor has been studied in stroke prevention, it's not recommended as monotherapy. Recent studies show benefit only when combined with aspirin, similar to clopidogrel-aspirin combination."}]},{"n":24,"q":"A 65-year-old male presents with sudden onset right-sided facial weakness and slurred speech that completely resolved within 45 minutes. Which of the following statements about this transient ischemic attack (TIA) is incorrect?","o":[{"t":"A) If neurological symptoms begin improving within 1 hour, it indicates TIA"},{"t":"B) Treatment window period from symptom onset is 4.5 hours"},{"t":"C) Patient has highest risk of developing stroke after 72 hours"},{"t":"D) Symptoms must completely resolve within 24 hours to be classified as TIA"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The highest chance of developing stroke after TIA is within the first 48 hours (not 72 hours). The risk of stroke is ~10-15% in the first 3 months, with most events occurring in the first 2 days."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Transient Ischemic Attack:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1. Timeframes:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms typically improve within 1 hour (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Complete resolution within 24 hours (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Highest stroke risk in first 48 hours"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treatment window: 4.5 hours (Option B)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2. Diagnosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical diagnosis primarily"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal NCCT does not rule out TIA"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"If infarction is seen on imaging, it's classified as stroke regardless of symptom duration"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"3. Symptoms (FAST criteria):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"F: Sudden onset Facial weakness"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A: Sudden onset of Arm weakness"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"S: Speech deficit"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"T: Time is neurons (better prognosis if symptoms are picked early)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"All symptoms occur irrespective of age group"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms are sudden in onset, not gradual"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Complete resolution should occur within 24 hours"}]},{"n":25,"q":"A 58-year-old male presents with acute left-sided weakness. MRI imaging is performed showing:  Which of the following best describes the significance of the diffusion-perfusion mismatch in this patient?","o":[{"t":"A) It represents chronic ischemic changes and is not clinically relevant"},{"t":"B) It indicates the ischemic penumbra and potentially salvageable tissue"},{"t":"C) It is a marker of hemorrhagic transformation risk"},{"t":"D) It represents a completed infarction that cannot be salvaged"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The diffusion-perfusion mismatch represents tissue that is at risk but not yet infarcted"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This region (penumbra) has decreased blood flow but preserved cellular integrity"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It identifies tissue that could potentially be saved with timely reperfusion therapy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This mismatch is a critical imaging biomarker for selecting patients for intervention"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In the above scenario,"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diffusion-weighted image (DWI) showing restricted diffusion in the right hemisphere"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Perfusion imaging showing a larger area of perfusion deficit"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The images show a significant mismatch between the DWI lesion and the perfusion deficit"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"MRI in Acute Stroke:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DWI shows areas of restricted diffusion (likely core infarct)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Perfusion imaging shows total area of compromised blood flow"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mismatch represents potentially salvageable tissue"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"More sensitive than CT for early infarct detection"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Can reliably document extent and location of infarction"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Helps identify stroke mimics"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Can detect hemorrhage using specific sequences"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Helps in patient selection for reperfusion therapies"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Particularly useful in wake-up strokes or unknown time of onset"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Can guide treatment decisions in extended time windows"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"More time-consuming than CT"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Less readily available"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"May not be suitable for critically ill patients"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Contraindicated in patients with certain metallic implants"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Acute Ischemic Stroke Imaging:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The primary goals of initial imaging are to:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1. Exclude hemorrhagic stroke"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2. Identify early signs of ischemia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"3. Rule out stroke mimics"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"4. Guide treatment decisions, particularly regarding thrombolysis and thrombectomy"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Non-contrast CT is the initial imaging modality of choice due to its speed, availability, and ability to rule out hemorrhage. However, it may not show ischemic changes in the first few hours after stroke onset."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Advanced imaging techniques :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CT perfusion can identify areas of reduced blood flow and potential salvageable tissue (penumbra)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CT angiography is useful for identifying large vessel occlusions and may be performed after the initial non-contrast CT, especially if mechanical thrombectomy is being considered."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MRI with DWI is highly sensitive for early ischemic changes and can detect small infarcts that may be missed on CT. It is time-consuming and may delay treatment. In the acute setting, the priority is to quickly rule out hemorrhage and other contraindications to thrombolysis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MR perfusion , when combined with DWI, can also identify the ischemic penumbra."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"It represents chronic ischemic changes and is not clinically relevant (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The DWI shows acute changes, not chronic"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mismatch specifically represents acute pathophysiology"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Helps in acute treatment decision-making"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"It is a marker of hemorrhagic transformation risk (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While hemorrhagic risk assessment is important, the mismatch pattern itself is not a direct marker"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"It represents a completed infarction that cannot be salvaged (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The DWI lesion represents the core infarct"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The mismatch area is specifically tissue that is not yet infarcted"}],"qi":[7472]},{"n":26,"q":"A 72-year-old male presents to the emergency department with sudden onset of left-sided hemiplegia and aphasia. His last known normal time was 5 hours ago. CT angiography reveals a right middle cerebral artery (MCA) occlusion. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?","o":[{"t":"A) Intravenous thrombolysis with tPA"},{"t":"B) Mechanical thrombectomy"},{"t":"C) Antiplatelet therapy only"},{"t":"D) Supportive care and observation"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mechanical thrombectomy is the most appropriate treatment for this patient. The patient presents outside the 4.5-hour window for intravenous thrombolysis, but within the 6-hour window for mechanical thrombectomy . CT angiography has confirmed a large vessel occlusion (right MCA), which is an indication for mechanical thrombectomy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mechanical Thrombectomy:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1. Need for Mechanical Thrombus Removal:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Some clots, especially platelet-rich ones, are resistant to thrombolysis due to high concentrations of PAI-1."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mechanical thrombectomy can quickly restore cerebral blood flow in cases where thrombolysis is contraindicated, has failed, or the patient presents outside the thrombolysis window."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2. Goal of Mechanical Clot Manipulation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"To rapidly restore cerebral blood flow in the affected arterial territory."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"To achieve functional recovery with reduced or no use of fibrinolytics."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"3. Indications:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Large vessel occlusion (e.g., internal carotid artery, M1 segment of MCA) confirmed by imaging."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Within 6 hours of symptom onset for anterior circulation strokes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Can be extended up to 24 hours in selected patients with favorable imaging (e.g., good collaterals on CT or MR perfusion)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Failed or contraindicated IV thrombolysis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"4. Procedure for Mechanical Thrombectomy:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vascular access via the femoral artery."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A guidewire and microcatheter are advanced to the site of occlusion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A retrievable stent (often made of nitinol) is deployed to ensnare the clot."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The stent with the entrapped clot is withdrawn under balloon protection to prevent distal embolization."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Intravenous thrombolysis with tPA (Option A): This is incorrect because the patient is outside the 4.5-hour window for IV tPA administration."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Antiplatelet therapy only (Option C): While antiplatelet therapy is part of secondary stroke prevention, it is not the most effective acute treatment for a large vessel occlusion within the time window for mechanical thrombectomy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Supportive care and observation (Option D): This is inadequate management for a patient with a large vessel occlusion who is within the time window for mechanical thrombectomy."}]},{"n":27,"q":"A 65-year-old male presents with sudden onset of vertigo, dysphagia, hoarseness, and left-sided facial numbness. On examination, he has left-sided Horner's syndrome, right-sided loss of pain and temperature sensation in the body, and left-sided cerebellar signs. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"A) Parinaud syndrome"},{"t":"B) Dejerine syndrome"},{"t":"C) Wallenberg syndrome"},{"t":"D) Weber syndrome"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation described is characteristic of lateral medullary syndrome, also known as Wallenberg syndrome . This condition is typically caused by occlusion of the vertebral artery or posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lateral Medullary Syndrome (Wallenberg Syndrome):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Involves the 5th, 9th, and 10th cranial nerves"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Vascular causes:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Occlusion of vertebral artery"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Superior, middle, or inferior lateral medullary arteries"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ipsilateral symptoms:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Contralateral symptoms: Impaired pain and temperature sensation in the body"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Parinaud syndrome (Option A):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stroke syndrome caused due to Pineal gland tumor- Pinealoma exerting pressure on superior colliculus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Upward gaze palsy - Sun setting sign."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Medial Midbrain Syndrome (Weber Syndrome): (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Involves the 3rd cranial nerve"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presents with ipsilateral oculomotor palsy and contralateral hemiplegia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Dejerine syndrome (Medial Medullary Syndrome): (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Involves the 12th cranial nerve"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presents with ipsilateral hypoglossal nerve palsy, contralateral hemiplegia, and contralateral loss of proprioception"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"4 Brainstem Rules of Four:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"4 Midline Structures (M's) - Motor tract, MLF, Medial Lemniscus, Motor CN nuclei fi Medial = MOTOR"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"4 Side (Lateral) Structures (S's) - Sympathetic, Spinocerebellar, Spinothalamic, Sensory CN 5 fi Side = SENSORY"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"4 CN in the Medulla, 4 CN in the Pons, 4 CN in the Midbrain"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"4 Motor Nuclei are divisible into 12 (3,4,6,12 = Motor, Medial) so (5,7,9,11 = Sensory, Side)"},{"k":"img","v":7535},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"4 Medial Structures (Overall: OPPOSITE Motor weak, vibrate, proprioception + same INO)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Motor Tract (Corticospinal) - Opposite weakness arm & leg"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Medial Lemniscus (Posterior Column) - Opposite vibration, proprioception arm & leg"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MLF (Median Longitudinal Fasciculus) - Same Look left (but right eye stays straight) or vice versa"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Motor CN Nuclei (divide into 12) - Midbrain (3,4) eye move, Pons (6) eye lateral, Medulla (12) tongue move"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"4 Side Structures (Overall: SAME Shaky leg, Horner's SRO, face sense + opposite pain/temp arm, leg)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sympathetic - Same Horner's SRO (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spinocerebellar - Same ataxia in arm & leg"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spinothalamic - Opposite pain/temp in arm & leg"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sensory CN 5 - Same face sensation"}]},{"n":28,"q":"A 65-year-old man with a history of hypertension presents with a sudden onset of right-sided weakness affecting his face, arm, and leg without any speech disturbance. CT scan shows a small infarct in the posterior limb of the internal capsule. Which of the following vessels is most likely involved in this pathology?","o":[{"t":"A. M1 segment"},{"t":"M2 segment"},{"t":"Lenticulostriate arteries"},{"t":"D. M3 segment"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The lenticulostriate arteries are penetrating branches arising from the M1 segment of the middle cerebral artery . They supply important deep structures including the internal capsule, putamen, and caudate nucleus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The described pure motor hemiparesis is a classic lacunar syndrome resulting from occlusion of a lenticulostriate artery affecting the posterior limb of the internal capsule."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lacunar strokes result from occlusion of small penetrating arteries and typically cause distinct clinical syndromes"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lacunar Strokes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Lacunar Location of Infarct Supplying Vessel Clinical Features Syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pure Motor Posterior limb of Lenticulostriate arteries or Face, arm, and leg Hemipare internal capsule or paramedian pontine weakness on one side sis basis pontis arteries without sensory, visual, or"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cognitive deficits"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pure Sens Ventral posterolateral Thalamogeniculate Hemisensory loss ory Stroke nucleus of thalamus arteries affecting face, arm, and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"leg without motor weakness"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ataxic Posterior limb of Lenticulostriate arteries or Ipsilateral ataxia with Hemipares internal capsule or paramedian pontine contralateral pyramidal is basis pontis arteries signs"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Dysarthria- Pons or internal Penetrating branches of Dysarthria and dyspraxia Clumsy capsule (genu) basilar artery or of hand without significant Hand lenticulostriate arteries weakness"}]}]);
QB.imgs({"7526":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7463":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7501":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7535":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7472":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7507":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7509":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7485":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"});